From 32df81744579acdf74a282cc8bc9590e3e4ba039 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 10:58:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] validation/tc-preinit-array: Make the ordering counter
 volatile

The test records the order in which the .preinit_array and .init_array
constructors ran by incrementing a shared counter.  A compiler is free to
evaluate the .init_array constructor at translation time and commit its
effect to the static initializer, and clang's GlobalOpt does exactly that
at -O2.  The .preinit_array constructor then observes the value the
.init_array constructor left behind, and the ordering the test validates
becomes unobservable: constructor_counter reads 2 and
normal_constructor_counter reads 1.

Making the counter volatile keeps the sequence a run time property.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 testsuites/validation/tc-preinit-array.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/testsuites/validation/tc-preinit-array.c b/testsuites/validation/tc-preinit-array.c
index 78606b80ba..7e706a77dd 100644
--- a/testsuites/validation/tc-preinit-array.c
+++ b/testsuites/validation/tc-preinit-array.c
@@ -84,7 +84,15 @@ static bool test_case_executed;
 
 static bool constructor_test_case_executed;
 
-static uint32_t counter;
+/*
+ * This counter records the runtime order in which the .preinit_array and
+ * .init_array constructors ran.  It must be volatile: otherwise a compiler may
+ * evaluate the .init_array constructor at translation time and commit its
+ * effect to the static initializer (clang's GlobalOpt does this at -O2).  The
+ * .preinit_array constructor then observes the post-constructor value and the
+ * ordering this test validates becomes unobservable.
+ */
+static volatile uint32_t counter;
 
 static uint32_t normal_constructor_counter;
 
